            <h3 class="text-lg font-serif-display font-bold text-maroon-deep flex items-center gap-2">
              <span class="text-vermilion">॥</span> What is the Hanuman Chalisa? (परिचय एवं इतिहास)
            </h3>
            <p>
              The <strong>Shree Hanuman Chalisa</strong> is a revered forty-verse devotional stotra composed in the 16th century by the celebrated Bhakti saint and poet <strong>Goswami Tulsidas</strong> (1532–1623 CE). Composed in melodic <strong>Awadhi</strong>—an Eastern Hindi literary language closely linked with Sanskrit—the word <em>Chalisa</em> derives from the Hindi word <em>chalis</em>, signifying forty. The hymn consists of two introductory couplets (dohas), forty rhythmic quatrains (chaupais), and a concluding blessing doha.
            </p>
            <p>
              According to devotional tradition, Tulsidas composed this sacred prayer while imprisoned in Fatehpur Sikri by the Mughal Emperor Akbar after refusing to perform an imperial miracle. Turning his complete surrender toward Sri Hanuman—the guardian deity of all Rama bhaktas—Tulsidas meditated upon Hanumanji&apos;s divine attributes, valor, and humble servitude. On the fortieth day of recitation, an extraordinary swarm of monkeys entered the citadel, leading to the saint&apos;s honorable release and immortalizing the forty chaupais as a protective armor for humanity.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="space-y-4">
